Chapter: GpuGaugeClamp
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites UI chapter Theme
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter BoxModel
  cites UI chapter GpuRender

 A gauge fills the fraction of itself that its value is of its maximum,
 so a value above that maximum is a fraction above one, and a fraction
 above one is a rectangle wider than the widget it belongs to. The
 framebuffer compositor has clamped since it was written. This backend
 did not, so the same widget tree painted differently depending on which
 renderer walked it, and on this one the difference lands on whatever
 sits to the right.

 Nothing already in the tree could see it. The triangle count cannot: an
 over-wide rect is submitted and counted exactly as a correct one is, and
 gpu-depth-tree already reads pixels but asks only whether the fill is
 present at all, which it is in both cases. What distinguishes them is
 WHERE the fill stops, so the instrument has to scan outside the widget.

 The tree is a gauge pinned to a fixed width with an empty label taking
 the rest of the row. A label emits text and no box, so nothing but the
 root panel paints in the band to the gauge's right, and the root panel
 paints its background and its border -- neither of which is pal-primary
 in theme-terminal. A pal-primary pixel out there can only be gauge fill
 that escaped.

 Both readings are printed because either alone is uninformative. The
 outside count answers the question; the inside count is the control that
 says the gauge painted at all, without which a renderer that drew
 nothing would report a clean outside and pass.

Section: Body

  fb-base : Integer = #BF000000
  fb-w : Integer = 640
  fb-h : Integer = 480

  scan-row-band : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  scan-row-band (y) (x) (xend) (target) (acc) =
    if x >= xend then acc
    else scan-row-band y (x + 4) xend target (acc + (if peek-32 fb-base ((y * fb-w + x) * 4) == target then 1 else 0))

  scan-band : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  scan-band (y) (x0) (x1) (target) (acc) =
    if y >= fb-h then acc
    else scan-band (y + 1) x0 x1 target (acc + scan-row-band y x0 x1 target 0)

 The value is half again its maximum, so an unclamped fill runs half the
 gauge's own width past its right edge.

  probe-tree : WidgetNode
  probe-tree =
    widget-panel "root" DirRow 0 [
      widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-gauge "over" 150 100) 200 40) 0,
      widget-set-flex (widget-label "pad" "") 1
    ]

  opening : [Gpu, Console] Nothing =
    act
      let th = theme-terminal
      in let pal = th.th-palette
      in let laid = widget-layout probe-tree th (layout-rect 0 0 fb-w fb-h)
      in let g = list-at (laid.wn-children) 0
      in let b = g.wn-bounds
      in let gright = b.lr-x + b.lr-w
      in act
        c1 <- gr-clear (pal.pal-bg)
        c2 <- gr-clear-depth
        n <- gr-flush ((gr-render-tree (gf-new 4096) laid th gr-depth-scene).gf-idx)
        print-line-uni ("inside: " & show (if scan-band 0 (b.lr-x) gright (pal.pal-primary) 0 > 0 then 1 else 0))
        print-line-uni ("outside: " & show (if scan-band 0 (gright + 4) (fb-w - 8) (pal.pal-primary) 0 > 0 then 1 else 0))
      end
    end
